1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,620
But it has a lot of absurd implications,

2
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:05,720
such as everyone would be happy to take a one

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,600
in a trillion gamble of getting a quadrillion dollars,

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,160
rather than get $500 for sure.

5
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,600
And almost no one wants deals like that.

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,180
I mean, when the numbers get that big,

7
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:18,180
it's a little confusing.

8
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:20,800
But you can see how many people would

9
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,000
want a one in 1,000 chance of a million dollars,

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,920
rather than, say, $500 for sure.

11
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,760
So it seems quite unlikely.

12
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:32,720
So we just have paper towel.

13
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,200
You can get that.

14
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:35,680
Can we get rid of this?

15
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:36,680
It'll work, I promise.

16
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:37,680
Oh, all right.

17
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:38,680
Thank you.

18
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:39,180
Hold on.

19
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,360
We're going to do this one.

20
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:40,860
Oh, great.

21
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:41,360
All right.

22
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:44,240
So anyway, expected value maximization

23
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:47,280
rejected for these obviously absurd conclusions.

24
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:49,680
And so then economists normally switch over

25
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:53,840
to the expected utility theory, which many people misinterpret

26
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:54,840
as being the same thing.

27
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,280
But it's totally not.

28
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:57,360
What's the big difference?

29
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:00,240
The big difference is that with expected utility theory,

30
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:04,000
we give everyone a utility of wealth function,

31
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
such as utilities equal to the square root of wealth.

32
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,440
And then people maximize the expected value of that.

33
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:12,840
You'll maximize the expected value of that.

34
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:16,320
And this is a way where you can easily

35
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:20,120
build in both risk aversion and risk preferences

36
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:21,840
we're going to see.

37
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:24,320
The procedure for expected utility theory.

38
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:28,600
Step one, you assign numerical waste outcomes via

39
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:31,680
an expected utility function.

40
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:35,760
Then linearly weight outcomes by the probability.

41
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:37,440
So it's a linear weight.

42
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:38,440
What else can you do?

43
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:40,360
In principle, you could go and weight everything

44
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:42,680
by the square of the probability.

45
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:44,400
Well, the square root of the probability.

46
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:48,240
These are all logically possible ways of doing it, right?

47
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,200
Still, you say, yeah, I just go and do

48
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:53,560
whatever has the highest value of the square

49
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:55,360
of the probability times utility function.

50
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,360
But that's not how expected utility theory works.

51
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:00,440
And then you just choose whatever gamble

52
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:05,160
has the highest linearly weighted expected outcome.

53
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,040
So like I said, if we have this function,

54
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,760
and you've got either a 50% chance of $10,000,

55
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:12,880
a 50% chance of zero, or 2,000 with certainty,

56
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:18,000
all that we do is go and do 0.5 times the square root of 10,000

57
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,200
plus 0.5 times the square root of zero,

58
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:25,720
and compare that to the 1 times the square root of 2,000.

59
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:28,480
And why 1 times the square root of 2,000?

60
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:32,880
Because the probability of that thing happening is 1.

61
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:37,200
Now, you might remember back when

62
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,360
you were learning basic utility theory,

63
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:43,840
and you heard that basic utility theory

64
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:46,680
is invariant up to any monotonic transformation.

65
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:50,320
So what that means is that for any regular utility function,

66
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:52,440
you're always going to have to square it.

67
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:53,200
You would cube it.

68
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,480
You would take the square root.

69
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,760
And it gives you exactly the same answer regardless.

70
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:00,640
Remember when you start off with a Cobb-Douglas function,

71
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:02,680
and then you take the log of it, and then it

72
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,160
turns into this other thing that looks very different?

73
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,800
And that's perfectly fine, because the log function

74
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,400
is monotonic.

75
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,600
Whatever number was higher before you logged it

76
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:16,760
is still higher after you logged it.

77
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:18,200
Number six?

78
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:19,160
Vaguely?

79
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:19,840
Vaguely?

80
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:20,480
All right.

81
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:22,560
Now, expected utility theory is not like that.

82
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,280
Expected utility theory, if you square it

83
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:26,800
or take the square root, it changes the answer,

84
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:28,720
which means that with expected utility theory,

85
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:31,440
there's extra information contained within that utility

86
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,680
function.

87
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,960
And all that you're allowed to do

88
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:37,460
to expect utility functions, you're

89
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:39,480
allowed to multiply them by a constant,

90
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:42,240
and you're allowed to add a constant.

91
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:44,360
Those are perfectly fine.

92
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:46,000
And there is a technical term.

93
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,160
This is called an affine transformation.

94
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:49,720
But don't worry about it.

95
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:53,560
The main thing to know is that expected utility functions

96
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,280
have extra information packed into them.

97
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:58,360
And so you're not mathematically allowed

98
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,120
to just square them or take the square root

99
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:01,780
and assume the answer will be the same.

100
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,480
In fact, that assumption would be wrong.

101
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:08,080
Now, so there are some implications

102
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:12,220
of expected utility theory that seem

103
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:15,560
pretty obvious and trivial, given the axioms.

104
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:18,400
And yet, if you remember what human beings are actually

105
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:23,600
like, and remember those creatures, human beings,

106
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:26,640
you may still feel like one after all this economics.

107
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:27,520
Or maybe you don't.

108
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:29,600
Maybe you now feel like you are superior

109
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,120
to regular human beings.

110
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:33,560
But either way, important to remember

111
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:35,760
that it's supposed to be a theory about them.

112
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:37,920
So if you remember these things, you'll

113
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:41,080
realize that these are actually problems.

114
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:45,680
So one implication is the compounding one,

115
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:48,600
which says that you'll be indifferent between a 50%

116
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:50,600
chance of a 50% chance of something

117
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:53,360
and a 25% chance of something.

118
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:56,280
And you say, well, everyone is like that, aren't they?

119
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:56,880
No.

120
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:58,800
Have you ever talked to a toddler?

121
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,640
Toddlers will sometimes, they won't

122
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:03,600
want to go and roll all the dice at once.

123
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:06,400
They'll want to first roll one, and then they, OK,

124
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,800
now I'm halfway there.

125
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:10,520
And then they want to roll the other one.

126
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,200
And you're like, what's the difference?

127
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:13,700
And they're like, well, I can't stand

128
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:16,000
to have it all determined at once.

129
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:17,480
It's too stressful.

130
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:19,000
You're like, it doesn't matter.

131
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,800
It's 25% either way.

132
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,440
I don't need to hear that.

133
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:26,680
Just give me, I'm going to roll one die at a time.

134
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,600
That's the only way I feel safe doing this.

135
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,040
I mean, it just seems like only kids would do this.

136
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:33,920
I've seen adults do the same way about it.

137
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:35,460
So if you think about this, if you're

138
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:37,400
at a casino in Las Vegas, can you

139
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:40,680
imagine someone flipping the cards over one at a time

140
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:43,000
instead of all at once?

141
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,680
That is a violation of compounding.

142
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,200
That's one where somehow they get

143
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:53,080
a weird sense of insurance out of this metaphysically

144
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:55,600
nonsensical procedure.

145
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:57,400
And but here's the thing.

146
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:00,440
If human beings act in a metaphysically nonsensical way,

147
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:03,520
then the model has got to account for that.

148
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:07,060
It's got to, yeah, well, the world can't have a contradiction,

149
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:09,280
but a person can contradict himself.

150
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,400
Perfectly fine.

151
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:15,280
Puzzle, like a human being being contradicted.

152
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:16,640
Now another implication.

153
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:20,880
This is one that is very important in the real world.

154
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,960
If you're selling insurance or if you

155
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:28,800
are trying to fear monger the population into voting for you,

156
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:31,960
there's the linear probabilities.

157
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:36,880
So with expected utility theory, a 1% chance of something

158
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:40,960
is just a little bit less, if it's a bad outcome.

159
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:44,000
Say a 1% chance of a bad outcome is a little bit worse

160
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,840
than a 2%, which is in turn just a little bit better.

161
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:51,280
And then in turn, so wait, hold on, this is a bad outcome.

162
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:55,120
So a 2% chance of a bad outcome is just a little bit worse

163
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:00,800
than a 1%, which in turn is a little bit worse.

164
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:02,480
OK, so bad outcome.

165
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:07,080
So 2%, this is worse, a little bit worse,

166
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:08,640
and then this is best.

167
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:09,560
But here's the thing.

168
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:11,440
So with expected utility theory, this is linear.

169
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:14,440
So moving from here to here should psychologically

170
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:16,280
be the same as moving from there to there.

171
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:20,320
Going from 2% to 1% is exactly the same as a move from 1%

172
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:21,720
to 0%.

173
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,240
And yet, if you are selling any kind of insurance product

174
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:26,680
to people, it is standard to try to say,

175
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:29,160
you are guaranteed this cannot happen anymore,

176
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:31,240
so you're going to stop worrying about it.

177
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:33,400
And that seems a lot more rhetorically persuasive

178
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:36,360
to people than saying, you know that 2% risk you face?

179
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:38,480
After this, we'll only get 1% risk.

180
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:40,160
But you're telling me there's a chance.

181
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:40,720
Yes.

182
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:42,600
Yeah, yeah, as long as there's a chance.

183
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:43,640
You're telling me there's a chance.

184
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:45,320
Now, but think about it a little bit more.

185
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:46,320
You say, but wait a second, that's

186
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:48,600
wrong because there's a chance the insurance company goes

187
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:49,960
bankrupt and doesn't pay me.

188
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:51,720
There's a chance that they rule that it doesn't really

189
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:54,200
count as the thing that I would think it would count as.

190
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:55,720
And so again, I don't get paid.

191
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:59,000
So you haven't really given me assurance, like a certainty

192
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,160
at all.

193
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:01,700
But if you are selling the insurance,

194
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:03,080
don't bring up that stuff.

195
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:05,480
Instead, just bring up, there'll be no chance.

196
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:07,480
You're totally safe.

197
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:09,760
Totally safe.

198
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:12,440
And again, if you are going and trying

199
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:16,920
to beer monger with a group of voters, what do you do?

200
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:19,680
You just say, look, we don't want the smoking gun

201
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,680
to be a mushroom cloud.

202
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,640
What probability here?

203
00:08:23,640 --> 00:08:26,520
Look, you just don't want that to happen at all.

204
00:08:26,520 --> 00:08:28,200
And this will vote for us, and it's zero.

205
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:29,800
So everything's fine here.

206
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:31,680
Now, you could question the empirics and say,

207
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:32,760
it's not really zero.

208
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:34,320
In any case, just the movement, like,

209
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:36,200
why is it so important to move it to zero

210
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:38,680
rather than one in a trillion?

211
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:40,760
It's almost the same thing, right?

212
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:43,040
You could live a million years, and you'll probably never

213
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:45,360
see a one in a trillion probability occur.

214
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:49,480
So why is zero so much discreetly better than one

215
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:51,040
in a trillion for people?

216
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:52,440
And yet, psychologically, it seems

217
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:53,560
that people are this way.

218
00:08:56,760 --> 00:08:59,440
And even for seemingly pretty trivial things,

219
00:08:59,440 --> 00:09:02,560
there's no chance you'll miss lunch if you do this.

220
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:04,400
How bad is that?

221
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:05,760
I've missed lunches before.

222
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:07,240
I survived.

223
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:10,520
Sometimes I barely even noticed until a few hours later.

224
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:13,920
But nevertheless, this putting great weight

225
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:18,080
upon certainties or zeros and treating those

226
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:20,600
as being different in kind from movements

227
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:26,400
within the one non-zero, non-one probability of another,

228
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:28,400
so the speculative theory says that won't happen.

229
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:30,480
And yet, it seems like in the real world,

230
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:33,840
people do think this way a lot.

231
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:35,320
All right.

232
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:37,960
So now this is saying that you value $1,000 100 times as much

233
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:38,720
as 10.

234
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,880
It's the probabilities that matter linearly.

235
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:43,440
So the rewards totally can matter non-linearly,

236
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:45,280
but not the probabilities.

237
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:51,080
And that's what the inverse of the equation is.

238
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:51,580
All right.

239
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:53,560
So any questions?

240
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:56,060
Questions?

241
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:57,040
All right.

242
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:59,320
So let's now do rational interpretations

243
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:00,880
in a river of generality.

244
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:01,960
Symmetric information.

245
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:03,920
Many of these models you may have heard before,

246
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:05,640
but usually they are not grouped together

247
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:06,840
as symmetric information.

248
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:08,720
It's common to group all the asymmetric information

249
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:10,960
together, but not the symmetric information together.

250
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:14,040
But it's worth thinking about it as,

251
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:15,720
that's why I do cluster them, that there

252
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:19,120
is a whole area of different models of information

253
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:20,880
where it is symmetric, where everyone is

254
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:24,800
in the same boat of ignorance.

255
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:28,280
Now, back in week one, seven weeks ago,

256
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:30,440
I believe that it's been that long.

257
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:32,400
All right, we went over two different versions

258
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,400
of probability.

259
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:35,960
And what are those?

260
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,200
Objective and subjective.

261
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:38,360
That's right.

262
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:40,760
All right, and subjective is much more generally applicable

263
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:42,280
than objective.

264
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:44,320
Subjective, you can have a subjective probability

265
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:45,120
about anything.

266
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:48,520
Things that are unique, things that have never occurred,

267
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:50,880
you can have subjective probabilities for those.

268
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:52,640
Objective probability, on the other hand,

269
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:54,200
is based upon observed frequency.

270
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:55,560
So it's never happened.

271
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:59,560
Then all you can say is 0 out of 0 times as it occurred.

272
00:10:59,560 --> 00:11:01,720
All right, so undefined.

273
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:04,920
Now, the problem, as we said, is that subjective probabilities

274
00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:08,840
have no necessary connection to the real world at all.

275
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:11,440
And this seems weird, because it does

276
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:13,680
look like there's some connection between the way

277
00:11:13,680 --> 00:11:16,080
the world really is and what people think.

278
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:18,880
Like, normally, if an extra country is formed,

279
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:22,120
then people start believing that country exists.

280
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:24,320
Or if there is mass hunger in the street,

281
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:26,720
people switch their view that there is now

282
00:11:26,720 --> 00:11:29,920
mass hunger in the street to some extent.

283
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:32,360
All right, so what can we do about this?

284
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:34,400
Well, the leading theoretical effort

285
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:37,360
to link objective and subjective probabilities

286
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,560
is called rational expectations, or just RE for short.

287
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:44,000
There are two different ways that rational expectations

288
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,320
generally is explained.

289
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:48,280
There is the one that is simplest

290
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:51,160
and is most commonly actually used for empirics.

291
00:11:51,160 --> 00:11:54,200
And this just says that you've got rational expectations

292
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:58,240
if your errors have a mean of 0 and are uncorrelated

293
00:11:58,240 --> 00:11:59,760
with available information.

294
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:02,320
So again, a standard characterization

295
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:05,040
of rational expectations is that your errors are distributed

296
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:08,480
normally with a mean of 0 and constant variance.

297
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:13,440
Then those errors are uncorrelated with each other.

298
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:16,960
Now, there is a little bit of weaseling here,

299
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:18,400
because we say it was uncorrelated

300
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:21,360
with available information, with what counts as available.

301
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:22,920
But when you're doing empirical work,

302
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:25,560
you normally say, well, it was in the Wall Street Journal

303
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:28,440
yesterday that it's available.

304
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:30,040
Why that?

305
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:33,000
That's normally the way that it's done in practice.

306
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,400
Now, there's a deeper version, which is primarily

307
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:39,400
used just by theorists, because you can't really

308
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:41,200
do much with it in the real world.

309
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:44,720
But this version just says that you have rational expectations

310
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:47,240
if your subjective probability distribution matches

311
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:51,000
the objective probability distribution in the real world.

312
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:52,540
This is really hard to actually test,

313
00:12:52,540 --> 00:12:56,520
but you can do it in a model very cleanly.

314
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:00,560
So basically, if you see a rational expectations model,

315
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:03,120
the normal approach to doing rational expectations

316
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:06,400
is you solve for the equilibrium where

317
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:08,240
the belief distribution exactly matches

318
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:09,720
the distribution of events.

319
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:11,520
And it all works out perfectly if you've

320
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:12,600
done the model correctly.

321
00:13:12,600 --> 00:13:17,960
All right, now, a standard way of modeling

322
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:21,120
is this, just to say that if rationalization is true,

323
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:23,400
then everyone has unbiased beliefs.

324
00:13:23,400 --> 00:13:25,840
So their mean error is zero.

325
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:29,520
And then what does it mean to have imperfect information

326
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,640
or error in a world of rational expectations?

327
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:34,320
There is a caricature that just says

328
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:35,800
people who believe in rational expectations

329
00:13:35,800 --> 00:13:37,040
think error doesn't exist and everyone

330
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:38,280
has perfect information.

331
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:39,960
That's totally wrong.

332
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:42,720
So wipe that thought from your consciousness

333
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:45,640
because it's utterly in error.

334
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:47,640
What rational expectation says is, sure,

335
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:49,160
there could be tons of error.

336
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:51,840
But the error has to look a certain way.

337
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:57,400
Namely, if this is the actual truth, T star,

338
00:13:57,400 --> 00:14:02,400
then the distribution of beliefs has to have a mean of zero.

339
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:06,360
So on S. So you're equally likely to be above or below

340
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:09,160
the actual true value.

341
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:11,800
I didn't even notice that over there.

342
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:14,080
So basically, it says that even though people

343
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:17,040
may have really big errors, and nonetheless,

344
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:20,360
their average belief is true.

345
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:22,320
Now, for one individual, this isn't that helpful

346
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:25,600
because for any given question, you just get one realization.

347
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:28,960
And then, well, I don't know, maybe I'm really wrong.

348
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:32,680
But I could just as easily be really high or really low.

349
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:34,440
So in a rational expectations world,

350
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:36,840
what does it mean to say that one person is well-informed

351
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:38,360
and one is poorly informed?

352
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:41,400
Well, a well-informed person is a small error

353
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:43,000
around the true mean.

354
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,480
That's a well-informed person.

355
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:49,720
And on the other hand, a poorly informed person

356
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:51,920
has a big distribution around the mean.

357
00:14:51,920 --> 00:14:55,120
So this is a poorly informed person.

358
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:57,400
However, on average, their views are the same.

359
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:00,120
It's just a difference of the spread.

360
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:03,080
So just by the definition of variance,

361
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:06,040
you can see that a poorly informed person on average

362
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:10,920
is much farther from the truth than a well-informed person.

363
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:13,720
And of course, a perfectly informed person has no error.

364
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:15,440
And then their belief distribution

365
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:19,760
just is a straight line overlapping the truth.

366
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:22,720
So that's what rational explanation says.

367
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:25,480
Again, it's not claiming that people are omniscient

368
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:27,720
or anything like that.

369
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:31,120
It's just saying that there is a very specific structure

370
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:36,120
to how error works, which is that no matter how oblivious you

371
00:15:36,120 --> 00:15:38,600
are, still, on average, you're right.

372
00:15:38,600 --> 00:15:40,880
And the difference between the well-informed and poorly

373
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:44,880
informed is just how far the scatter around the truth is.

374
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:50,240
And furthermore, of course, rational explanation

375
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:53,080
does not assume that information or cognition is free.

376
00:15:53,080 --> 00:15:55,160
In fact, if it were free, you'd expect

377
00:15:55,160 --> 00:15:58,520
that there'd be no error, right?

378
00:15:58,520 --> 00:16:00,360
And essentially, if you were to go and raise

379
00:16:00,360 --> 00:16:02,000
the price of information, you'd expect

380
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,400
that this would increase the error.

381
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:06,000
So it's a pretty simple story.

382
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,120
The greater the price of information

383
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:10,960
is, the less effort you're going to put into acquiring more

384
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:12,840
and reducing the error, and then the greater

385
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:14,520
the expected spread.

386
00:16:14,520 --> 00:16:16,480
On the other hand, when information gets cheap,

387
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:19,520
then you'd expect that beliefs will squeeze in

388
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:22,680
towards the center.

389
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:26,640
And you can apply this idea to any number of possibilities.

390
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:28,560
Like it would be for attending graduate school.

391
00:16:28,560 --> 00:16:32,360
So no one knows for sure how well they'll do.

392
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:36,080
But if rational expectations hold, then on average,

393
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:37,680
you do know the truth.

394
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:39,320
On average, you know the truth.

395
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:41,320
And you also know what completion

396
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:42,560
will do for you, and so on.

397
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:43,060
Yes?

398
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:46,400
So if information isn't free, and different people

399
00:16:46,400 --> 00:16:48,400
have different information, then are we just

400
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:51,160
saying that people are on average well-informed,

401
00:16:51,160 --> 00:16:53,520
or on average poorly informed?

402
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:56,400
Well, here's the thing, is that for any one belief,

403
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:58,640
every individual only gets to really well to die once.

404
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:03,000
So here's the thing.

405
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:04,560
For one individual, it's super hard

406
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,040
to find out whether they satisfy the rational expectation

407
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:07,600
assumption.

408
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:10,240
Just like if you only have one observation,

409
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,200
how do you statistically test anything?

410
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:14,800
So in practice, if you wanted to test rational expectations,

411
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:17,240
you would wind up going and getting a big group of people

412
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:19,880
and seeing whether the average belief winds up

413
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:22,200
being zero, which is an implication

414
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:23,280
of rational expectations.

415
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:24,960
But there is another possibility.

416
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:26,960
It could just be that everyone's bias is biased,

417
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:28,440
but there's different biases.

418
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:30,240
But then how do you prevent, I guess,

419
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:32,520
skew from appearing as the cost of permissions

420
00:17:32,520 --> 00:17:34,960
that increase or decrease?

421
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:37,080
So the answer, how do we prevent skew?

422
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:38,240
We assume it away.

423
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:40,520
That's what it does.

424
00:17:40,520 --> 00:17:43,320
Again, you might go, so now people that are really into it

425
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:47,480
say, well, why would we ever think there would be any skew?

426
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:49,080
And so, you don't have an answer?

427
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:51,240
All right, well, we don't have to worry about it.

428
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,400
Of course, when you think about it a bit more,

429
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:53,400
there's lots of reasons.

430
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:56,760
What if the truth is bounded by zero?

431
00:17:56,760 --> 00:17:59,440
How many hamburgers did we eat yesterday?

432
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:02,200
It can't actually be a normal distribution

433
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:04,720
because there's a minimum of zero hamburgers.

434
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:06,800
You can't eat negative hamburgers.

435
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:09,000
So there's a problem there.

436
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,440
And the actual amount of effort, intellectual effort

437
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:12,840
that's spent on things like that,

438
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:14,200
throwing it out when you're zero,

439
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:17,560
that's a pretty obvious issue.

440
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:19,180
Or you could do it for renting a movie.

441
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:22,360
Or actually now, since no one rents movies,

442
00:18:22,360 --> 00:18:24,800
you do it for Netflix ratings.

443
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:29,960
So on average, are Netflix ratings accurate for you?

444
00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:31,640
And there, of course, if one individual

445
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:33,120
had to give a bunch of observations for yourself,

446
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:34,520
you can determine, are they accurate for me,

447
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:35,840
on average, at least?

448
00:18:35,840 --> 00:18:37,320
Or you can do a bigger one and see,

449
00:18:37,320 --> 00:18:39,880
are they accurate for people overall?

450
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:42,720
Or something else you could do is

451
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:44,840
you could go and do a bunch of guesses

452
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:46,420
on many different issues, and then you

453
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:48,520
could test yourself for actualizations.

454
00:18:48,520 --> 00:18:50,240
You can't do it just for one observation

455
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:53,080
because then how do you know that it wasn't just random error?

456
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:54,600
But if you do a lot.

457
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:56,240
In fact, this would be a useful thing

458
00:18:56,240 --> 00:18:57,720
to know about yourself, right?

459
00:18:57,720 --> 00:19:00,720
How accurate am I, really?

460
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:03,640
And there is the crummy way of determining your own accuracy,

461
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:06,360
which is to wait for things to happen and then self-assess.

462
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:06,960
Was I right?

463
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:10,200
Oh, yeah, totally, totally, always so right.

464
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:13,280
So Philip Tetlock calls this self-scoring.

465
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:15,280
And it's corrupt, and you shouldn't do it.

466
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:16,600
It's nice to do it.

467
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:19,240
People that do this all the time and don't

468
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:21,960
seem to have any interest in coming up with some other thing,

469
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:24,040
there's many alternatives to self-scoring.

470
00:19:24,040 --> 00:19:25,600
One of them is just to state what you

471
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:27,200
and your prediction clearly in advance

472
00:19:27,200 --> 00:19:29,040
and then come back later.

473
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:31,560
So then at least you don't wait for things to happen and go,

474
00:19:31,560 --> 00:19:32,960
ah, just as I foresee.

475
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:39,680
You say, well, why do people do self-scoring if it's so crummy?

476
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:42,960
Well, it's very easy to do well with self-scoring.

477
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:47,080
In fact, I don't know anyone whose self-score is that.

478
00:19:47,080 --> 00:19:49,720
There must be someone out there who's said, oh, my god,

479
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:52,520
I'm so terrible.

480
00:19:52,520 --> 00:19:55,840
And then even with making your predictions in advance,

481
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:58,240
there's a problem that you can go and pick

482
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:18,400
a bunch of really easy questions.


